The logarithm function
Before turning to the , it is important to remember what is a logarithm. A logarithm, defined in a certain basis \(a\), is the function
\[\begin{equation}
\text{log}_{a}(x) = y
\end{equation}\] that satisfies : \[\begin{equation}
a^y = x.
\end{equation}\]
where a&gt;0. As such, \(\text{log}_a(x)\) is only defined for \(x&gt;0\) values. For instance, the 10-basis logarithm of 1000 is: \[\begin{equation}
\text{log}_{10}(1000) = 3
\end{equation}\] because \[\begin{equation}
10^3 = 1000
\end{equation}\] The 2-basis logarithm of 4 is: \[\begin{equation}
\text{log}_{2}(4) = 2
\end{equation}\] again because : \[\begin{equation}
\text{log}_{2}(4) = 2
\end{equation}\]
The e-basis (Euler’s number) logarithm is generally called \(\text{ln}\), and gives :
\[\begin{equation}
\text{ln}(e) = 1
\end{equation}\] and \[\begin{equation}
\text{ln}(1) = 0
\end{equation}\]
As such, the logarithm of 1 is always 0, for any basis \(a\) : \[\begin{equation}
\text{log}_{a}(1) = 0
\begin{equation}

